# =============================================================================
# Environment template for the CRM TimeSheet project.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory and replace the placeholder
# values. `.env` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
#
#   Windows (PowerShell):  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   Linux / macOS:         cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` automatically because it sits next to
# `docker-compose.yml`. The NestJS backend reads it through @nestjs/config.
#
# The backend validates the variables it uses at startup and refuses to boot
# when one is missing or malformed — see
# `backend/src/config/env.validation.ts`.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL — consumed by the `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml.
#
# IMPORTANT: POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and POSTGRES_DB are read by the
# postgres entrypoint on FIRST boot only, when it initialises the data
# directory. Changing them later has no effect while the named volume
# `crm-timesheet-postgres-data` still exists — see README "Data persistence".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required. Database superuser created on first boot.
POSTGRES_USER=crm_user

# Required. Password for POSTGRES_USER. Use a strong value; never commit it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace_with_strong_password

# Required. Default database created on first boot.
POSTGRES_DB=crm_timesheet

# Optional. Host port the container's 5432 is published on, bound to
# 127.0.0.1 only. Change it if a local PostgreSQL install already owns 5432.
# Defaults to 5432 when unset.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required by the backend. PostgreSQL connection string.
# Host is `localhost` because the backend runs on the host during development.
# It becomes `postgres` (the Compose service name) once the backend itself
# runs as a Compose service. Keep user/password/db in sync with the values
# above, and with POSTGRES_PORT if you changed it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://crm_user:replace_with_strong_password@localhost:5432/crm_timesheet?schema=public"

# Optional. Runtime environment for the backend. One of `development`, `test`
# or `production`. Defaults to `development`.
NODE_ENV=development

# Optional. HTTP port the NestJS backend binds to. Integer between 1 and 65535.
# Defaults to 3000.
PORT=3000

# Optional. Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the API
# (CORS). Each origin must match scheme + host + port exactly — no trailing
# slash, no path:
#
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,https://crm.example.com
#
# `*` allows any origin; credentials are then disabled automatically, because
# browsers reject credentialed requests against a wildcard.
#
# When unset or empty, no browser origin is allowed — the safe default, so a
# misconfigured environment fails closed. Non-browser clients (curl, Postman,
# server-to-server calls) send no Origin header and are never affected.
#
# The value below is the Vite dev server default, i.e. the local frontend.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP — read by the backend's email module, and by nothing else.
#
# All of it is optional: with no mail server configured the backend still
# starts, `GET /api/v1/email/health` answers
# `{ "configured": false, "enabled": false, "connection": "NOT_CONFIGURED" }`,
# and any attempt to send is refused with a 500 rather than failing silently.
# That is the intended state on a machine that has no mail server.
#
# To actually send, five of them are required: SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_USER,
# SMTP_PASSWORD and SMTP_FROM_EMAIL. Miss one and the module reports itself as
# unconfigured — half a configuration is not a configuration. A variable set to
# nothing (`SMTP_HOST=`) counts as missing.
#
# These live here rather than in the database because they are a property of the
# deployment, not of the company: staging and production talk to different mail
# servers with different credentials, and SMTP_PASSWORD is a secret, which a
# table read by the application is not the place for.
#
# Verify a configuration with:
#   GET  /api/v1/email/health   — connects and authenticates, sends nothing
#   POST /api/v1/email/test     — { "email": "you@example.com" }
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Master switch. `false` makes the backend drop every message instead
# of sending it — notifications are silently skipped and the action that
# triggered them still succeeds. Set it on any environment that holds real
# employee addresses and must not mail them, such as a staging copy of
# production. `POST /api/v1/email/test` is the exception and refuses to run,
# because a test that sends nothing would be answering a question it did not
# ask. Defaults to true.
# SMTP_ENABLED=true

# Required to send. Hostname of the SMTP server.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com

# Required to send. Port, 1-65535. 587 for STARTTLS (the usual choice), 465 for
# implicit TLS, 1025 for a local Mailpit / MailHog.
# SMTP_PORT=587

# Optional. `true` opens the connection with TLS from the first byte, `false`
# starts in the clear and upgrades with STARTTLS. Only `true` and `false` are
# accepted. When unset it is derived from the port — true for 465, false for
# everything else — which is the right answer for every common server, so leave
# it alone unless yours is unusual.
# SMTP_SECURE=false

# Required to send. Account the backend authenticates as. For a provider like
# SendGrid this is a literal token name (`apikey`) rather than an address.
# SMTP_USER=no-reply@example.com

# Required to send. Password or API key for SMTP_USER. A secret — never commit
# it, and never reuse a person's mailbox password here.
# SMTP_PASSWORD=replace_with_smtp_password

# Optional. Display name shown to recipients, e.g. "HR Management System"
# <no-reply@example.com>. Without it the address appears on its own.
# SMTP_FROM_NAME=HR Management System

# Required to send. Address every message is sent from. Most providers require
# it to be a domain you have verified; a mismatch is a common cause of mail
# being accepted and then silently discarded.
# SMTP_FROM_EMAIL=no-reply@example.com

# Optional. Address replies should go to when that is not SMTP_FROM_EMAIL —
# typically a mailbox somebody reads, since the sending address usually is not.
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=hr@example.com

# Optional. Milliseconds to wait for the connection to the mail server, minimum
# 1000. Sending happens inside an HTTP request, so this bounds how long a
# request can be held open by an unreachable host. Defaults to 10000.
# SMTP_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT=10000

# Optional. Milliseconds an established connection may stay silent, minimum
# 1000. Covers the rest of the conversation, including a server that accepts the
# message and then thinks about it. Defaults to 30000.
# SMTP_SOCKET_TIMEOUT=30000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notification Delivery Engine — read by the notification-delivery module only.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional, defaults to true. Whether this deployment runs the engine's two
# scheduled jobs: the per-minute check for campaigns whose `scheduledAt` has
# arrived, and the daily check for reminder rules that are due.
#
# It does NOT switch the engine off — POST /api/v1/notification-delivery/execute
# /:campaignId still sends, because that is somebody deliberately asking. It
# stops the clock.
#
# Set it to false on any deployment holding a copy of production data. A staging
# database carries real employees, real addresses and real scheduled campaigns,
# and SMTP_ENABLED=false is not enough on its own: an in-app notification is
# delivered whether or not mail is configured.
#
# The WebSocket layer has no switch of its own and needs none — a gateway with
# nobody connected does nothing — and it reuses CORS_ORIGINS above rather than
# keeping a second allowlist.
# NOTIFICATION_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database seeding — read by `backend/prisma/seed.ts` only, never by the
# running application.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Password given to every account created by `npm run prisma:seed`.
# When unset, the seed uses the development default documented in
# `FEATURES/005-database-seeding.md`, which is published in this repository.
#
# Set it to anything else whenever the database is reachable by someone other
# than you — a shared development server, a demo environment, a teammate's
# machine. The seed refuses to run at all when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# Changing it only affects accounts the seed creates. Existing accounts keep
# their current password; `npm run prisma:reset` recreates them from scratch.
# SEED_PASSWORD=
